\text{Question}
Knuth densest sections of database searching in idealized universes \cite{knuth2004art} describes perfect matchings in complete graph where each edge is labeled by a positive integer. Suppose we have a complete graph with $N$ vertices such that all pairs of the same weight have at least a unique edge. If we consider each vertex as a node and its label as field id we can represent this graph as a vector of integers \lstinline|weights| such that $weights(i) = weight(i,j)$ if there is an edge from $i$ to $j$ and zeros otherwise.

Given a list \lstinline|sequences| such that each item is a permutation of \lstinline|weights|, we can re-index the elements of \lstinline|weights| with the corresponding elements of \lstinline|sequences|. Each entry can have negative values if any permutation has an edge from a lower weighted node to a higher weighted one. The number of such pairs is the number of \emph{inversions} of the sequence. We can easily find all possible sequences of \lstinline|weights| and calculate the number of inversions using various algorithms such as BubbleSort, QuickSort, or Mergesort.

To avoid generalization, let's take $N=8$ and four sequences as input.

\begin{verbatim}
weights = [5, 1, 6, 3, 2, 8, 7, 4]
sequences = permute([5, 1, 6, 3, 2, 8, 7, 4], k=4)
[permutation for permutation in sequences]

# as example output (each permutation is created using cycle shift operator in Python)
[[5, 1, 6, 3, 2, 8, 7, 4],
 [5, 6, 1, 3, 2, 8, 7, 4],
 [5, 6, 3, 1, 2, 8, 7, 4],
 [5, 6, 3, 2, 1, 8, 7, 4]]
\end{verbatim}
Find and write the code to calculate the number of inversions for each sequence and output the lists in the form provided below.
\begin{verbatim}
[[number of inversions for permutation 0],
 [number of inversions for permutation 1],
 [number of inversions for permutation 2],
 [number of inversions for permutation 3]]
\end{verbatim}

\hline

\textbf{\color{red}{Solution} - \color{ForestGreen}{Roszal}}
\begin{verbatim}
import itertools as it
def num_inversions(weights):
    n = len(weights)
    pairs = [() for _ in range(n)]
    for i, w in enumerate(weights):
        index = i + 1
        while pairs[index - 1]:
            j, jw = pairs[index - 1]
            if w < jw:
                pairs[index - 1] = ()
                break
            index += 1
        pairs[index - 1] = (i, w)
    return sum(1 for a, b in pairs if a > b)
permutes = lambda x, p=range(len(x)): list(it.permutations(x, p))
sequences = permutes(weights)
inversions = [[num_inversions(weights[i]) for i in inds] for inds in sequences]
print(inversions)
\end{verbatim}
