\end{align*}

Now we can find the discriminant of the quadratic equation:

\begin{align*}
\Delta &= (\sqrt{2} \cos 7 \theta)^2 - 4 \\
&= 2 \cos^2 7 \theta - 4 \\
&= 2(1 - \cos^2 7 \theta) - 4 \\
&= 2 - 2 \cos^2 7 \theta
\end{align*}

Now we need to investigate the roots of the quadratic equation based on the value of the discriminant:

1. If $\Delta > 0$, then there are two distinct real roots.
2. If $\Delta = 0$, then there is a repeated real root (a double root).
3. If $\Delta < 0$, then there are no real roots (complex roots).

